@* NumberField's Overview, written by hand after TextField's and the selection Overviews: limits
   from the model (previewed in each mode), decimals and units, step buttons, empty values, and
   states. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A number field is a native number input for a value the user types, such as a quantity or a price. It posts with the form, the browser checks its limits before the form post, and phones show a number keyboard. For a value the user judges by eye rather than types, such as a volume, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/slider">Slider</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Limits from the model</Heading>
<TextBlock>Limits set the lowest and highest values the field accepts. Set <Code>[Range]</Code> on the property, or <Code>Min</Code> and <Code>Max</Code> on the field.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="number-field-limits" />
<TextBlock>The browser checks the range before the form post, and the validator checks it again on the server. Once <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor> runs, the browser shows the attribute's <Code>ErrorMessage</Code>. Without an <Code>ErrorMessage</Code>, it shows the browser's default message.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>On static pages, a posted value that isn't a number binds as empty, null or 0, with no message of its own. Only a crafted form post can send one. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Decimals and units</Heading>
<TextBlock>A number field can accept decimals, and it can show a currency or unit beside the value. To allow decimals, set <Code>Step</Code> to the smallest increment, such as <Code>0.01</Code> for cents. To show a currency or unit beside the value, set <Code>StartAddon</Code> or <Code>EndAddon</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="number-field-decimals" />
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>Whole-number types step by 1. Decimal types allow any value unless you set <Code>Step</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The browser rejects a value between steps, with its default message.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The value is written and read with a dot, such as <Code>49.99</Code>, whatever the page's culture.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Add-on text isn't part of the field's name for screen readers. When the unit matters, put it in the label too, such as "Discount (%)".</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Step buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>Step buttons increase and decrease the value by one step. By default they're stacked at the end of the field. To split them across both ends, or leave them out, set <Code>StepButtons</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="number-field-steppers" />
<TextBlock>IzzyBlazor's script adds the buttons and hides the browser's own spin buttons. The buttons stop at each end of the range, and holding one down repeats the step. The Up and Down arrow keys step the value with or without them.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Empty values</Heading>
<TextBlock>To let the field be left empty, bind a nullable type, such as <Code>int?</Code>. An empty field then posts as null, and <Code>[Required]</Code> makes a value required.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Empty</Code>
<TextBlock>A field bound to <Code>int</Code> can't be empty. On static pages, an emptied field binds as 0. On interactive pages, it shows "The Quantity field must be a number."</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, read-only and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>To line the field up with buttons and other fields, set <Code>Size</Code>. For a value the user can see but not change, set <Code>ReadOnly</Code>; it still posts. For a value that doesn't post, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="number-field-states" />
<TextBlock>Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>. Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The step buttons have names for screen readers. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>IncreaseLabel</Code></td><td>The name of the button that steps up</td><td>"Increase"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>DecreaseLabel</Code></td><td>The name of the button that steps down</td><td>"Decrease"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* Label from [Display(Name)], limits from [Range]. *@
        <NumberField @bind-Value="Model!.Quantity" />

        @* Cents, with the currency attached. *@
        <NumberField @bind-Value="Model!.Price" Step="0.01">
            <StartAddon>$</StartAddon>
        </NumberField>

        @* Buttons at both ends, for a small count. *@
        <NumberField @bind-Value="Model!.Guests" StepButtons="StepButtons.Split" />
        """;

    private const string Empty = """
        @* Empty means "no limit": null when it posts empty. *@
        <NumberField @bind-Value="Model!.Seats" Placeholder="No limit" />

        @* Needed: a nullable type starts empty, and [Required] asks for it. *@
        <NumberField @bind-Value="Model!.Quantity" />

        @code {
            public sealed class Order
            {
                [Range(1, 500)]
                public int? Seats { get; set; }

                [Required, Range(1, 99)]
                public int? Quantity { get; set; }
            }
        }
        """;
}
